families.chart-computation-sign-twist-br.formal:
Face space has a concrete realisation: the three $\omega$-spinors lie in the two-dimensional $S_{\omega}$, so the face vector of pairwise brackets $\Omega = ([\omega^{2}\omega^{3}], [\omega^{3}\omega^{1}], [\omega^{1}\omega^{2}])$ is the vector of $2 \times 2$ minors of a $3 \times 2$ array. Three consequences.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{(The sign twist.)} Under an odd permutation of the spinor labels, $\Omega \mapsto -P\,\Omega$: the face vector transforms in $\mathbf{3} \otimes \mathrm{sgn} = \mathrm{sgn} \oplus \mathbf{2}$. The democratic direction is the sign representation, invariant as a line and odd as a vector, consistent with Proposition \claimref{families.alignment-computed-thirteenth-refutation-democracy-one}'s language (a direction, not a vector) and tying the line's two orientations to the $S_{3}$-odd structure the ordering must break.
\item \emph{(The bracket form, and what it selects.)} The complex-bilinear form on face space built from the symplectic $\varepsilon$ on the spinor slots and the Kronecker $\delta$ on the family slots is $C(\Omega) = \sum_{k}\Omega_{k}^{2}$ (the letter is the series' for this form; it is not the Casimir $C$ of Fact \claimref{families.imported-dictionary-exact-join}), the Cauchy-Binet norm of the minors. It is $S_{3}$-invariant, and it is the member $\alpha = 1$, $\beta = -1$ of Proposition \claimref{families.koide-nullity-condition-one-member-invariant}'s family: on a real face vector $C(v) = s^{2} + |d|^{2} = |v|^{2}$, Euclidean, and $C(v_{\mathrm{lepton}}) = \sum_{k} m_{k} = 1883$ MeV, not zero. The $S_{3}$-invariant tensors on the family indices are $\delta_{kl}$ and the all-ones $J_{kl}$, so $C$ is singled out among the invariant forms only by excluding $J$, that is by requiring invariance under $O(3)$ on family indices rather than $S_{3}$, which nothing in the paper supplies. On the complexified doublet $C$ is nondegenerate, so, like every nondegenerate two-dimensional complex quadratic space, it is a hyperbolic plane: $C(f_{\pm}) = 0$ and $C(f_{+}, f_{-}) = 3$ for the Fourier vectors $f_{\pm} = (1, \zeta^{\pm 1}, \zeta^{\mp 1})$, $\zeta = e^{2\pi I_4/3}$ (numbers and the module's complex structure are read in $\mathrm{span}\{1, I_4\}$, the module read on the tower's floors, where $I_4$ commutes with the tower vectors), and $C(af_{+} + bf_{-}) = 6ab$, so its null vectors are exactly the two Fourier lines. Those lines contain no nonzero real vector: $C$-nullity and the $q$-nullity of Proposition \claimref{families.koide-nullity-condition-one-member-invariant} share nothing but the word.
\item \emph{(The Fourier coordinates, and the one number.)} The map $\Gamma \mapsto (\mathrm{Re}(\Gamma\zeta^{k}))_{k}$ is an $\R$-linear bijection from $\mathrm{span}\{1, I_4\}$ onto the real doublet plane $\{d : \sum_{k} d_{k} = 0\}$, so every real face vector has the form
\begin{equation}
\sqrt{m_{k}} \;=\; a + |\Gamma|\cos\!\Bigl(\delta + \frac{2\pi k}{3}\Bigr), \qquad \delta = \arg\Gamma,
\end{equation}
three real parameters for three components: the classic Koide parametrisation, with its $2\pi/3$ phase spacing, is the Fourier coordinate system on $\mathbb{Z}/3$ and carries no hypothesis. In these coordinates the relation's entire content is one number: $Q_{\sqrt{m}} = \tfrac23 \iff |\Gamma|^{2} = 2a^{2}$, and the charged-lepton data give $|\Gamma|/a = 1.4142$ against $\sqrt{2} = 1.4142$ (the script certifies $|\Gamma|^{2}/a^{2} = 6Q_{\sqrt{m}} - 2$ inside $2 \pm 10^{-4}$), with $\delta = 0.222$ the free phase that encodes $m_{\mu}/m_{e}$, for the labelling $k = 0, 1, 2 = \tau, e, \mu$. The normalisation $|\Gamma|^{2} = 2a^{2}$, the one number, is the section “The Gaussian theorems”'s question.
\end{enumerate}

families.doublet-direction-module-georgi.formal:
Let $\chi := -(-1)^{N_{R}}$, which is $+1$ on the doublet sector $N_{R} = 1$ and $-1$ on the singlet sectors $N_{R} \in \{0, 2\}$, and let
\[
T_{\mathrm{GJ}} \;:=\; w_{4}\,(B - L)\,\chi .
\]
Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $B - L = -\tfrac23\sum_{i \le 3} v_{i}\wedge w_{i}$ is pure grade two, $w_{4}\,\chi = w_{4}(1 - 2n_{5}) = -2\,w_{4}\,(v_{5}\wedge w_{5})$, and
\[
T_{\mathrm{GJ}} \;=\; \tfrac43\; w_{4}\wedge (v_{5}\wedge w_{5}) \wedge \Bigl(\sum_{i\le 3} v_{i}\wedge w_{i}\Bigr),
\]
a pure grade-five element of the Clifford algebra of the ten, with no grade-one and no grade-three component; hence, by Fact \claimref{families.imported-decomposition-grade-dictionary}, it has no $10$ and no $120$ component, and the mass form it induces on the sixteen is the $126$ component of $16 \otimes 16$, the $\overline{126}$ part of the grade-five space acting as zero on the sixteen;
\item[(ii)] under Hypothesis (S), which supplies the species names, $Y_{T_{\mathrm{GJ}}}$ is symmetric, conserves hypercharge, and is nonzero exactly on $(L, e^{c})$ with entries $-1$ and on $(Q, d^{c})$ with entries $\pm\tfrac13$, in the normalisation in which the $10$ form $Y_{w_{4}}$ has entries $\pm1$ on the same pairs; writing $y_{e}$, $y_{d}$ for the entries of $Y_{T_{\mathrm{GJ}}}$ on $(L, e^{c})$ and $(Q, d^{c})$, the ratio $|y_{e}/y_{d}| = 3$ is Georgi-Jarlskog's, and it is the ratio of the lepton doublet's to the quark doublet's $B - L$;
\item[(iii)] the one-sided weighting $w_{4}(B - L)$ without $\chi$ is antisymmetric on every coupled pair and contributes no one-family mass, because every Yukawa pair is $B - L$ neutral, $(B-L)(L) + (B-L)(e^{c}) = 0 = (B-L)(Q) + (B-L)(d^{c})$;
\item[(iv)] under Hypothesis (S) as in (ii), the up-type direction $v_{4}(B - L)\chi$ has a symmetric mass form coupling $(L, \nu^{c})$ with modulus $1$ and $(Q, u^{c})$ with modulus $\tfrac13$, so that $|m_{\nu}^{D}| = 3\,|m_{u}|$ on the $126$ direction; the signed entries are $+1$ on $(L, \nu^{c})$ and $\pm\tfrac13$ on $(Q, u^{c})$, the sign depending on the colour of the pair, so the relation is one of magnitudes.
\end{enumerate}

families.faces-ordered-what-identificati.formal:
Two results move Conjecture \claimref{families.three-generations-faces-maximal-composite}.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{(The missing ingredient identified, at structural level.)} Proposition \claimref{families.three-identical-families-mixing-shared-slots} makes the three faces $S_{3}$-identical, while physical families differ by mass alone; identifying faces with families therefore requires $S_{3}$ to break. The framework's only $S_{3}$-charged vacuum object is the face-valued bracket: the mass bracket is face-valued, one bracket per two-spinor face, so the vacuum of Propositions \claimref{families.bracket-fixed-join} and \claimref{families.source-term-linear-tilt-mass-functional} is a direction in face space, and its alignment spontaneously breaks $S_{3}$, ordering the faces by their overlap with it. The face identification is not an additional postulate awaiting new structure: it is the condensate's alignment, and what that expectation value does and does not determine is exactly what Proposition \claimref{families.alignment-computed-thirteenth-refutation-democracy-one} states.
\item \emph{(The first numerical test, at observation level.)} Single-slot overlap with an aligned condensate is a nearest-neighbour Yukawa texture: direct transitions between the two faces not sharing the condensate's slots are doubly suppressed. The classic consequence of such textures is the Cabibbo relation $\theta_{C} \approx \sqrt{m_{d}/m_{s}} \approx 0.22$, against the observed $0.225$: with $m_{d} = 4.70$, $m_{s} = 93.5$ MeV \cite{PDG2024} the square root is $0.224$, and the conjecture's corollary shape, confronted with its first number, passes at the percent level the quark-mass uncertainties allow. The test inherits the standard assumptions of the texture literature and is flagged at that level; its failure at precision would have refuted the corollary, and did not.
\end{enumerate}
The residue is the alignment's anisotropic part, which depends on the one expectation value Proposition \claimref{families.source-term-linear-tilt-mass-functional} names.

